ance → TCP LDP session → remote service-label binding','Remote label → TCP session → IP route → targeted Hello','Pseudowire MAC table → BGP UPDATE → targeted Hello → route','STP root → LDP label → IP route → peer acceptance'],0,'Targeted discovery and transport must exist before label exchange, and service state consumes the resulting label binding.','Follow the targeted-LDP control chain in dependency order.','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/ios/ios_xe/mpls/configuration/guide/convert/mp_ldp_book_xe/mp_ldp_overview_xe.html'),
('Cisco Systems','EIGRP OTP','architecture','H_EIGRP_OTP','Why can an ISP core remain unaware of enterprise EIGRP prefixes in an OTP design?',['Enterprise control reachability stays in EIGRP overlay while LISP carries data between OTP endpoints over transport IP','The ISP learns every enterprise prefix through hidden BGP communities','EIGRP replaces the provider FIB directly','OTP requires one shared Layer-2 VLAN through the ISP'],0,'OTP separates enterprise EIGRP routing from provider transport and uses LISP encapsulation for data.','Provider reachability to locators is required; enterprise prefix visibility is not.','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/routers/ios-xe/ip-routing/b-ip-routing/m_ire-eigrp-over-the-top.html'),
('Palo Alto Networks','ECMP Symmetric Return','packet reasoning','H_PAN_SYM_RETURN','A server flow enters ethernet1/1 and two equal-cost reply routes exist. With Symmetric Return enabled, what path decision is overridden?',['Normal ECMP hashing for the server-to-client reply, which is forced back out the ingress interface','BGP best-path selection before routes enter the RIB','OSPF DR election','The source IP chosen for firewall-originated IKE'],0,'PAN-OS Symmetric Return is a session return-path override, distinct from Strict Source Path for local IKE/IPsec.','Identify which packet direction and traffic origin the feature governs.','https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/ecmp/configure-ecmp-on-a-virtual-router'),
('Palo Alto Networks','OSPF MD5 Key Preference','configuration review','H_PAN_OSPF_MD5','Two OSPF MD5 keys are configured and Key ID 20 is the top Preferred key. Which key signs outgoing packets?',['Key ID 20','The numerically lowest key ID','Both keys concatenated','A key selected from the neighbor router ID'],0,'PAN-OS uses the top Preferred MD5 key for outgoing OSPF authentication.','Key preference is explicit profile ordering, not numerical selection.','https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/advanced-routing/create-ospf-routing-profiles'),
('Palo Alto Networks','BGP TCP-MD5','state machine','H_PAN_BGP_MD5','Repeated TCP SYNs to port 179 never lead to OpenSent on an authenticated BGP peer. Which boundary comes first?',['TCP-MD5 authentication of the transport session','LOCAL_PREF comparison','Route aggregation suppression','BGP ORF prefix filtering'],0,'BGP OPEN occurs after TCP establishes, so transport authentication must succeed first.','Use the FSM evidence: the failure is pre-OPEN.','https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/advanced-routing/create-bgp-routing-profiles'),
('Fortinet','RIPng metric 16','protocol mechanics','H_RIPNG16','A FortiGate receives a RIPng route with metric 16. How is it interpreted?',['Unreachable/poisoned','Sixteen equal-cost paths','Connected IPv6 route','Default route request'],0,'RIP/RIPng use metric 16 as infinity/unreachable.','Metric 16 is not a valid forwarding hop count.','https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.4.10/administration-guide/146217/basic-ripng-example'),
('Fortinet','PIM policy vs RPF','scenario diagnosis','H_FORTI_PIM_POLICY','PIM neighbors and RP mapping are correct, RPF is correct, but FortiGate still denies multicast crossing policy boundaries. What remains?',['A matching multicast firewall policy','A route-server-client neighbor role','RIPng split horizon','BGP graceful shutdown'],0,'FortiGate multicast control state and firewall multicast policy are independent gates.','Correct PIM/RPF does not bypass security policy.','https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.6.6/administration-guide/896987/multicast-routing-and-pim-support'),
('Fortinet','BGP route-server semantics','architecture','H_FORTI_RS','Why is a route-server client not equivalent to a route-reflector client?',['Route-server semantics distribute eBGP-style client reachability without iBGP RR cluster behavior','Route servers always rewrite routes into OSPF','Route reflectors never carry next hops','Route servers require all clients in one VRF-Lite VLAN'],0,'FortiOS exposes route-server-client and route-reflector-client separately because their BGP semantics differ.','Do not translate route-server and RR scaling models as synonyms.','https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.4.8/cli-reference/225427711/config-router-bgp'),
('Arista Networks','BGP-LU explicit-null','packet reasoning','H_BGPLU_EXPLICIT','An EOS egress advertises IPv4 explicit-null label 0 for a local BGP-LU prefix. What does the penultimate LSR do?',['Keeps label 0 on the packet so the egress performs ultimate-hop popping','Pops all MPLS labels before the egress','Replaces label 0 with an EVPN VNI','Treats label 0 as unreachable'],0,'Explicit-null requests that the label remain to the egress instead of PHP.','Label 0 is a valid IPv4 explicit-null instruction.','https://www.arista.com/en/support/toi/tag/bgp-labeled-unicast'),
('Arista Networks','BGP-LU LSP OAM','command/output','H_BGPLU_OAM','IP ping to the egress loopback works but MPLS traceroute for the LU FEC fails at hop 2. What is localized?',['The labeled forwarding path for that FEC fails at or before hop 2','The egress loopback lacks an IP route','The BGP TCP session is necessarily down','The tenant EVPN RT is missing'],0,'LSP traceroute validates the MPLS path rather than generic IP reachability.','A working IP path is not proof of a working LU LFIB path.','https://www.arista.com/en/support/toi/tag/lsp-ping'),
('Arista Networks','LDP pseudowire','architecture','H_PW_LABEL','In a conventional LDP pseudowire, what distinguishes the remote customer circuit from the MPLS transport path?',['The pseudowire/service label','The customer source MAC alone','The outer IP TTL','An EVPN Route Distinguisher'],0,'Transport label gets traffic to the PE; the service label identifies the pseudowire attachment circuit.','Separate transport LSP and service-label roles.','https://www.arista.com/en/support/toi/author/108-coutinho'),
('FRRouting (FRR)','SHARP testing','command/output','H_SHARP','SHARP submits 10,000 synthetic routes through one nexthop. The nexthop disappears. Which evidence proves kernel installation status rather than mere ZAPI submission?',['zebra/kernel install notifications and the Linux route table','BGP OPEN counters','PIM RP mapping','EIGRP feasibility condition'],0,'SHARP requests routes through zebra; zebra still resolves and programs the kernel.','Submission and successful FIB programming are separate stages.','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/sharp.html'),
('FRRouting (FRR)','MGMTd transactions','state machine','H_MGMTD','A route-policy edit exists in MGMTd candidate but COMMIT_CONFIG has not run. Which statement is correct?',['Running/backend protocol state need not contain the edit yet','bgpd must immediately apply candidate edits','zebra installs a route at SET_CONFIG time','Candidate and running are always identical'],0,'MGMTd separates candidate staging from coordinated commit to running/backend daemons.','Candidate intent is not applied state.','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/mgmtd.html'),
('FRRouting (FRR)','EIGRP zebra dependency','architecture','H_FRR_EIGRP_ZEBRA','eigrpd still has a successor but zebra is stopped. What failure is expected?',['Protocol topology can remain while normal RIB/kernel integration is unavailable','DUAL writes routes directly into Linux without zebra','Every EIGRP adjacency must instantly fail','MGMTd converts the successor to static'],0,'FRR eigrpd depends on zebra for interface/RIB/kernel integration.','Protocol computation and system forwarding installation belong to different daemons.','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/stable-9.1/eigrpd.html'),
('Juniper Networks','BGP Add-Path','capability negotiation','H_J_ADD_PATH','R1 is configured Add-Path send, but R2 does not advertise receive capability. What should R1 do?',['Send ordinary best-path behavior rather than multiple Add-Path paths','Send four paths because local send overrides capability','Reset the session with prefix-limit error','Convert alternatives to OSPF routes'],0,'Add-Path direction is negotiated; sender requires the peer’s complementary receive capability.','Local configuration is not enough without negotiated capability.','https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/cli-reference/topics/ref/statement/add-path-edit-protocols-bgp.html'),
('Juniper Networks','OSPF HMAC-SHA2 keychain','scenario diagnosis','H_J_OSPF_CLOCK','Two routers have identical OSPF